[[:db/retract 17592186412933 :answer-field/choices 17592186412927] [:db/retract 17592186412958 :answer-field/choices 17592186412876] {:db/id 17592186151846 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[5]_{8}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $5$ modulo $8.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5]_{8} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=5+8k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{5+8k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,5-2\\cdot 8,\\,5-8,\\,5,\\,5+8,\\,5+2\\cdot 8,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-11,-3,5,13,21,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $13$ belong to $[5]_{8},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151848 :question/worked-solution "We have $[a]_{3} = [b]_{3}$ if \n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:3).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[13]_{3}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $13$ modulo $3.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[13]_{3} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\cdot 3+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\cdot 0+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\} \\\\  & =[1]_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412976 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[7]_{11}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $7$ modulo $11.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[7]_{11} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=7+11k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{7+11k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,7-2\\cdot 11,\\,7-11,\\,7,\\,7+11,\\,7+2\\cdot 11,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-15,-4,7,18,29,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $18$ belong to $[7]_{11},$ while $15$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412964 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[4]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $4$ modulo $7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4]_{7} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=4+7k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{4+7k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,4-2\\cdot 7,\\,4-7,\\,4,\\,4+7,\\,4+2\\cdot 7,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-10,-3,4,11,18,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-3$ and $18$ belong to $[4]_{7},$ while $6$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412972 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[-7]_{11}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-7$ modulo $11.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-7]_{11} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=-7+11k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{-7+11k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,-7-2\\cdot 11,\\,-7-11,\\,-7,\\,-7+11,\\,-7+2\\cdot 11,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-29,-18,-7,4,15,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $15$ belongs to $[-7]_{11},$ while $-4$ and $7$ do not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412971 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[9]_{13}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $9$ modulo $13.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9]_{13} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=9+13k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{9+13k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,9-2\\cdot 13,\\,9-13,\\,9,\\,9+13,\\,9+2\\cdot 13,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-17,-4,9,22,35,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-17$ and $35$ belong to $[9]_{13},$ while $4$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152792 :question/worked-solution "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412967 :question/worked-solution "We have $[a]_{8}=[b]_{8}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-17]_{8}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-17$ modulo $8.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-17]_{8} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-17\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-3)\\cdot 8+7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-3)\\cdot 0+7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\} \\\\  & =[7]_{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412974 :question/worked-solution "We have $[a]_{7}=[b]_{7}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[16]_{7}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $17$ modulo $7.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16]_{7} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡16\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\cdot 7+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\cdot 0+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\\} \\\\  & =[2]_{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412960 :question/worked-solution "We have $[a]_{6}=[b]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-26]_{6}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-26$ modulo $6.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-26]_{6} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-5)\\cdot 6+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-5)\\cdot 0+4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =[4]_{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412962 :question/worked-solution "We have $[a]_{5}=[b]_{5}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-13]_{5}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-13$ modulo $5.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-13]_{5} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-3)\\cdot 5+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-3)\\cdot 0+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\} \\\\  & =[2]_{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412959 :question/worked-solution "We have $[a]_{6}=[b]_{6}$ if \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\nThe residue class $[-21]_{6}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $-21$ modulo $6.$ \n\nWe can find an alternative representation for this residue class as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-21]_{6} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡-21\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-4)\\cdot 6+3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡(-4)\\cdot 0+3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\\} \\\\  & =[3]_{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412973 :question/problem "Let $[\\,i\\,]_{223}$ denote the residue class modulo $223$ containing the element $i\\in Z.$ Select the correct options in the statements below.\n\n1. $\\,$ {{field-1}} $[\\,i\\,]_{223}=$ {{field-2}}\n\n2. $\\,$ $[\\,21\\,]_{223}$ {{field-3}} $[\\,162\\,]_{223}=$ {{field-4}}\n\n3. $\\,$ {{field-5}} $=\\{[\\,i\\,]_{223}\\,∣\\,${{field-6}} $\\le ${{field-7}} $<223\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. ${⋃}_{i=0}^{222}\\,[\\,i\\,]_{223}=Z$\n\n2\\. $[\\,21\\,]_{223}\\cap [\\,162\\,]_{223}=∅$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{223}=\\{[\\,i\\,]_{223}\\,∣\\,0\\le i<223\\}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412977 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $7≡2\\,(\\text{mod }5),$ we have that $[7]_{5}=[2]_{5}.$ Therefore, $[7]_{5}\\cap [2]_{5}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9]_{4} & =[1+4\\cdot 2]_{4}=[1]_{4} \\\\ [15]_{4} & =[3+4\\cdot 3]_{4}=[3]_{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{4}\\cup [9]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [15]_{4} & =[0]_{4}\\cup [1]_{4}\\cup [2]_{4}\\cup [3]_{4} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412963 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. $[0]_{12}\\cap [6]_{12}=∅.$\n\n2\\. $Z=[0]_{12}\\cup [1]_{12}\\cup ⋯\\cup [11]_{12}.$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{12}=\\{[0]_{12},[1]_{12},⋯,[11]_{12}\\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412933 :answer-field/correct "answer-afb9573a-26ee-54c8-92a6-fba0686fc18d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-afb9573a-26ee-54c8-92a6-fba0686fc18d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afb9573a-26ee-54c8-92a6-fba0686fc18d" :answer/id #uuid "afb9573a-26ee-54c8-92a6-fba0686fc18d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[0]_{12}\\,,[1]_{12},…\\,,[11]_{12}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412961 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are exactly $6$ distinct residue classes modulo $6.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $5≢1\\,(\\text{mod }3),$ we have that $[5]_{3}\\cap [1]_{3}=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2} & =[1+2\\cdot 1]_{2}=[1]_{2} \\\\ [6]_{2} & =[0+2\\cdot 3]_{2}=[0]_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3]_{2}\\cup [6]_{2} & =[1]_{2}\\cup [0]_{2} \\\\  & =[0]_{2}\\cup [1]_{2} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412968 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. There are $11$ distinct residue classes modulo $11.$\n\n2\\. $[5]_{11}\\cap [10]_{11}=∅.$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{11}=\\{[0]_{11},\\,[1]_{11},\\,…,\\,[10]_{11}\\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186412958 :answer-field/correct "answer-b1c150e2-8be3-50f4-b14c-159b0449160d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1c150e2-8be3-50f4-b14c-159b0449160d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1c150e2-8be3-50f4-b14c-159b0449160d" :answer/id #uuid "b1c150e2-8be3-50f4-b14c-159b0449160d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[0]_{11},[1]_{11},…,[10]_{11}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}]
